t the initial ideal of the defining ideal Image$J_{P,Q}$ of Image$K[P,Q]$ is squarefree. By a theorem of Sturmfels [Reference Sturmfels17, Chapter 8] this would imply that Image$K[P,Q]$ is normal, and by a theorem of Hochster [Reference Hochster13, Theorem 1] this in turn implies Cohen–Macaulayness. In Theorem 5.5 we show that if Image$P$ is a chain and Image$Q$ is a rooted or co-rooted poset, then Image$J_{P,Q}$ has a quadratic (and hence also a squarefree) Gröbner basis with respect to the reverse lexicographic order induced by a canonical labeling of the variables. In general, Image$J_{P,Q}$ may contain generators of arbitrarily high degree. This happens if Image$Q$ contains as an induced subposet, what we call a poset cycle. On the other hand, we conjecture that Image$J_{P,Q}$ is quadratically generated if and only if Image$Q$ does not contain any induced poset cycle of length greater than 4.\n\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n2  J.L. GROSS, T. MANSOUR, T.W. TUCKER, AND D.G.L. WANG (https://www.cs.columbia.edu/~gross/research/LocalLC-GMTW-EuroJC-2016.pdf)\nciteturn1search14 [wordlim: 200] Published: 10.9 years ago; We formulate Conjecture 2.2, a topological conjecture, called the *Local Log-Concavity Conjecture*. ... Section 4 gives an elementary proof of local log-concavity for vertices of degree four or less.\n\nSection 2 introduces the concepts of the *local genus polynomial* and *local log-concavity*. We formulate Conjecture 2.2, a topological conjecture, called the *Local Log-Concavity Conjecture*. This conjecture has a purely combinatorial equivalent form, given in Section 3 as Conjecture 3.1. Section 4 gives an elementary proof of local log-concavity for vertices of degree four or less. Section 5 examines how to use Jackson’s formula [Ja87] in explorations of the log-concavity of local genus distributions. Section 6 shows how the concept of *synchronicity*, introduced previously by the present authors [