roof of the general conjecture has turned out during the past decades to be quite hard. Most of the proofs of log-concavity for special families of graphs are based on partitioning of the genus polynomial according to the incidence of face-boundary walks on the root-vertices. Recently, Gross et al. [14] designed a new two-step strategy toward a proof of the general LCGD conjecture based on a new form of 2010 Mathematics Subject Classification. 05C10, 05A20. Key words and phrases. cyclic permutation, Hultman number, log-concave sequence, the Hermite–Biehler theorem. 1 2 J.L. GROSS, T. MANSOUR, THOMAS W. TUCKER, AND D.G.L. WANG partition of the genus polynomial into the set of local genus polynomials at a single root-vertex. The first step is to prove the log-concavity of the local genus polynomials of every rooted graph (G, v). The genus polynomial of the graph G is the sum of the local genus polynomials at v. This first step has been transformed into the purely combinatorial CLLC conjecture, which we describe in the next section. The second step is to prove the existence of an ordering of local genus polynomials subject to the synchronicity relation that was introduced in [13]. Completion of the above two steps together would confirm the LCGD conjecture. The exposition here is intended to be accessible both to topological graph the- orists and to combinatorialists. For additional contextual information regarding log-concavity and genus distributions, see [13]. General background information regarding topological graph theory can be found in the books [3, 16]. For surveys of log-concavity see [7, 8, 20]. 1. Local Genus Polynomials and IO-Polynomials This paper is devoted to extending the classes of cases for which the CLLC Conjecture is affirmed, that is, for which the local genus polynomials are known to be LC. We introduce the following notations. Sn : the symmetric group on n objects. c(π): for π ∈ Sn , the number of cycles of π. Qn : the set of n-cycles of Sn . G